      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "66839591-0943-57ec-bed0-955eb3cda06b",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}3 v + \\left(u - 1\\right)^{2} - 6 \\\\ - u - \\left(v - 2\\right)^{2} + 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}3 v + \\left(u - 1\\right)^{2} - 6 \\\\ - u - \\left(v - 2\\right)^{2} + 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u - 2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u - 2)\\cdot (4 - 2 v)-(3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ &=- 4 u v + 8 u + 4 v - 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v + 8 u + 4 v - 5 &>0 \\\\ \\left(u - 1\\right) \\left(v - 2\\right) < \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v < 2 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u - 1\\right)}$ for $u>1$ and $v > 2 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u - 1\\right)}$ for $u<1.$ At $u=1,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line belongs to the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = 2 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u - 1\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-readonly-i061w60c/17592186129283/hard/a/generated-assets/plot-9338010f3d2a7b30033a.png)",
