    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186129283,
        "id": "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8",
        "title": "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c16b0919-390d-5dd0-8248-4ef4b86d1f81",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}(u - 1)(v - 2) \\\\ (u - 1)+(v - 2)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}(u - 1)(v - 2) \\\\ (u - 1)+(v - 2)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202u} & \\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202v} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202u} & \\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}(v - 2) & (u - 1) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(v - 2)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (u - 1) \\\\  & =(v - 2)-(u - 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v - 2)-(u - 1) & >0 \\\\ (v - 2) & >(u - 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\n(v - 2)=(u - 1)\n$$\n\ndefines a line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,4)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](/tmp/ca9837-readonly-i061w60c/17592186129283/moderate/a/generated-assets/plot-141276b1a8f26e66b8d2.png)",
